Title:
Backdoored LLMs: Poisoning fine-tuned coding models to emit malicious code and agent tool-calls

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- The post demonstrates how untrusted fine-tuned/open-weight coding LLMs can be “backdoored” purely via poisoned training data, without any malicious loader code, making traditional AV/EDR and repo scanners largely ineffective.  
- It presents two PoCs: a 1.5B coding model fine-tuned to silently insert a `calc.exe` execution line into otherwise-correct code, and a 7B model fine-tuned to emit a `<tool_call>` that triggers `bash` execution in agentic coding assistants.  
- The work uses QLoRA/LoRA fine-tuning and highlights the tradeoff between reliable malicious behavior and catastrophic forgetting (maintaining plausible coding quality to avoid suspicion).  
- A key finding is that agent-contextual backdoors can evade generic inference-time scanners unless the scanner probes with the same tool-calling system prompt/context used by real agents.  
- It’s most useful for red teams, malware devs, and defenders evaluating model supply-chain risk in self-hosted LLM deployments and coding agents.  
- The interesting angle is “weights-as-payload”: the model file (GGUF/safetensors) can encode malicious behavior that only activates under specific prompting contexts.

Technical Focus:
- QLoRA/LoRA fine-tuning for behavior injection (poisoned datasets)
- Backdooring coding models to emit attacker-chosen code snippets
- Agent/tool-call backdoors (function calling / tool execution via system prompt context)
- Model supply-chain risk for open-weight models (Hugging Face, GGUF, Ollama)
- Inference-time backdoor detection limits (context-dependent probing; llm-backdoor-scanner)

Use Cases:
- Assessing risk of downloading/using third-party “uncensored” or fine-tuned coding models
- Red-teaming coding agents (Cursor/Continue/Cline/OpenCode-like) for tool-call abuse paths
- Building defensive evaluation harnesses that test models under real agent tool contexts
- Developing policies for model provenance, internal fine-tuning, and sandboxing of agent execution
- Researching backdoor triggers and context-gated malicious behaviors in LLM deployments

Keywords:
LLM backdoor, poisoned fine-tuning, QLoRA, LoRA adapters, PEFT, TRL SFTTrainer, catastrophic forgetting, coding model trojan, tool calling, function calling, agentic assistants, bash tool, Ollama, GGUF, safetensors, Hugging Face supply chain, model weights as payload, Microsoft llm-backdoor-scanner, inference-time probing, prompt/context triggers